                         Vendik's Tale - Part 2
                         [Possogroenoe's Folly]

                            by Alan Schwartz


    The caves stretched out extensively, and I felt ready to meet them.
My first few encounters with the denizens of the deeps had left a trail
of dead newts and those strange flickering bugs. I had even begun to see
the rewards of my labors. In a large room which held a bubbling
fountain, someone had left some coins scattered on the floor and I
rushed to pick them up. A bit more searching revealed an even more
valuable find, a crumpled scroll labeled "Prirutsenie".
    Checking my pouch again, I reviewed the scrolls my master had given
me: a spell to conjure up a monster, a spell to tame the monster, and
a blank scroll. This new parchment resembled none of these, and, feeling
plucky, I decided to read it.
    As I cast the spell, I could feel an insight dawning on me.
The old man had told me that Incantations are typically inscribed in
three parts, an evocation of the spirits, an expression of intention,
and the actual magic words. It was clear to me from the intention that
the spell should be cast on an item, and would identify the properties
of that item. I thought about the things I was holding, and quickly came
up with that strange magenta liquid of which Virgil had been so wary.
Finishing the spell, I proudly said to Virgil, "Oh, this? Why it's your
usual cursed potion of object detection. We wizards see these all the
time." Virgil did not deign to respond.
    The next cavern yielded a broken set of stone steps leading down
still further, and, with Virgil by my side, I descended once more.
I had but little time to look around this new room when a knife flew
past me. I looked down at the large black blade, and up again at a
squat, foul-smelling creature who was advancing on me. From my master's
bestiary, I knew this horrific visage could only be that of a goblim,
and, steeling myself, drew my athame and prepared for battle.
    The fight was over quickly. As the goblin and I grappled, nicking
one another with our blades, Virgil lept at his face and he fell
screaming to the floor. A moment later, he was no longer screaming, and
Virgil fed herself on his still-warm flesh.
    From afar I heard a voice, mumbling. As I wandered through the
narrow passageways, it grew louder, becoming a litany of swearing.
I stepped through an open doorway and the source of the voice became
clear.
    "By Loki, those damned pickpockets and thieves!" Noticing me, the
woman's scowling face perked up a bit. "Ah, hello adventurer. Welcome
to Possogroenoe's general store."
    I looked about in confusion. The large room was indeed a shop, and
scattered about were all manner of useful items. My eyes immediately
fell on a balsa wand in a display case at one end of the room. I knew
how valuable it might be, and wondered how I could acquire it, having
so little gold. I wandered over to the case and hefted the wand.
"600 coins for that, sir," Ms. Possogroenoe shouted. Far too expensive,
I thought.
    It was Virgil who once again came to my aid. Following me into the
shop, she starting running through the place, pawing at the
merchandise and throwing the shopkeeper into a rage. While Ms.
Possogroenoe chased my cat about, I grabbed the wand, and spelled out
my name in the dust of the floor, hoping to gain some knowledge of
the wand's working. But no such knowledge was vouchsafed to me.
In desperation, I wrote my name again, and to my surprise, the previous
inscription vanished without a trace. This, I knew, must be a wand
of great power, but I still could not afford to purchase it or even
to pay for a demonstration. I dropped it sadly on the ground and
returned to the shop door.
    Possogroenoe was busily tidying up her wares, when Virgil did an
amazing thing. The cat quietly crept over to the wand, and batted it
toward where I was standing. Insight dawned on me. "That's right,
Virgil," I whispered, "Bring it here, kitty." Moments later, the wand
was in my hands, and Possogroenoe had not noticed a thing. I resolved
to find Virgil a good meal soon.
    It was time for a real experiment, I thought, trying to remember
how my master conducted his magical studies. Who should I point
the wand at? Me? I quickly dismissed that thought. Aiming an
untried wand at myself would be the height of folly. Virgil was also
out of the question. And Possogroenoe was too dangerous. "Don't mess
with shopkeepers," Rentar had warned me once, "Or you'll get the
Guild after you." There seemed only one choice. Returning to the shop,
I aimed the wand at a vellum scroll sitting on a shelf, and waved it.
    Vanished! The scroll, too, disappeared without a trace. As I ran my
hands over the shelf where it had once stood, I puzzled again.
I had heard of spells of banishment, whereby people could be sent
to magical prisons or even hell itself. But were there not also spells
which moved things from place to place? Which, which?
    I left the shop with Virgil and had not gone 30 feet when before me
I found the vellum scroll, the words 'Andova Begarin' on the seal.
Now I knew. I smiled, and returned to the shop.


